Subject: DXVK: compile new graphics pipelines in the background (DXVK_ASYNC=1)
Date: 2026-10-24
Applies to: sources/dxvk (DXVK 1.10.3 cxaddon), after 0002
Files changed: src/dxvk/dxvk_pipemanager.h, src/dxvk/dxvk_pipemanager.cpp,
  src/dxvk/dxvk_graphics.h, src/dxvk/dxvk_graphics.cpp, src/dxvk/dxvk_context.cpp,
  src/dxvk/dxvk_memory.cpp, src/d3d9/d3d9_device.cpp, src/util/util_stall.h (new)

DXVK 1.10 compiles a graphics pipeline on the rendering thread the first
time a draw needs it. On MoltenVK each one is a SPIR-V to Metal conversion
plus a Metal compile, and Black Ops froze for a moment on every new effect.

With DXVK_ASYNC=1, a pipeline that does not exist yet is queued to two
low-priority threads in the pipeline manager, and getPipelineHandle()
returns VK_NULL_HANDLE, which makes the context skip that draw; the next
draw with the same state finds the pipeline once it is built. Each queued
state is recorded so it is only queued once. Built pipelines go to the state
cache as before, so later runs precompile them while loading. The compiler
threads are stopped with the state cache workers and before the pipelines
are destroyed.

The rendering thread only takes a separate lock for the list of queued
pipelines: the pipeline lock can be held for a whole compile by a state
cache worker, and a first version that took it still froze the game. The
background compile runs without that lock and publishes its result under
it.

Diagnostic, off unless DXVK_STALL_LOG=1: StallTimer (util_stall.h) logs
every call over 5 ms that can block a frame: pipeline lookup, graphics
state commit, shader creation, memory allocation, resource waits, CS thread
synchronisation and Present.

Cost: an object can be missing for the frames its pipeline takes to build.
Same idea as the dxvk-async patch for DXVK 1.x, written for this tree.
